<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.10.5: Solving Quadratic Equations by Any Method</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02260</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.10.5: Solving Quadratic Equations by Any Method</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6b01204b-0061-4b2b-b828-df78cb233fb5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Now, combine your new understanding of factoring with using the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">zero product property</term> to solve equations.</para>
          <para id="para-00002">Solve the equation \(2x^2− 7x + 5 = 0\) by any method. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Enter the solution to the equation \(2x^2− 7x + 5 = 0\) and your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\(x=\frac {5}{2}\) (or 2.5) and \(x=1\). Rewriting the expression in factored form gives \((2x − 5)(x − 1) = 0\). Using the zero product property: \(2x − 5 = 0\) or \(x − 1 = 0\), so \(x=\frac {5}{2}\) and \(x = 1\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00006">Building Character: Curiosity</para>
  <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_12_12_01.png"/>
          </media>

            <para id="para-00007">People typically spend more time learning about things they are curious about and more readily remember the information. Curious people tend to be happier individuals.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Think about your current sense of curiosity. Are the following statements are true for you?</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
          <item> When I didn’t know the answer to a question, I couldn’t rest until I figured it out. </item>
          <item> I explored a completely new idea or topic—just for the fun of it. </item>
        </list>
            <para id="para-00009">Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
